<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 2600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puabi was a queen or noblewoman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> around 2600 BCE, known from her richly furnished tomb in the Royal Cemetery, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a> in 1927–1928. A cylinder seal names her with the title nin, 'lady' or 'queen'. She was buried with a headdress of gold leaves, jewellery of lapis lazuli and carnelian and dozens of attendants, and her finds are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puabi.jpg" alt="Puabi" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nic McPhee from Morris, Minnesota, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Puabi_with_attendants.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Puabi lived in the Sumerian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> during its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a>, from about 2590 to about 2550 BCE. Leonard Woolley misread her name as Shubad or Shudi-Ad. Several cylinder seals in her tomb, grave PG 800 of the Royal Cemetery, give her the title nin or eresh, a Sumerian word for a queen or a priestess, so her status is somewhat in dispute. Her seal does not link her to any king or husband, which may mean she ruled in her own right, though it has also been suggested that she was the second wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a>. Her name might mean word of my father or orchard of my father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her tomb was among about 1,800 graves in the Royal Cemetery, excavated between 1922 and 1934 by a joint team of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the University of Pennsylvania Museum. It was unique for the number and quality of its goods and because looters had never touched it. They included a heavy headdress of gold leaves, rings and plates, a lyre with a bearded bull's head of gold and lapis lazuli, gold tableware, necklaces and belts of gold, carnelian and lapis lazuli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> adorned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> lioness heads.</p><p>She was buried with 52 attendants, among them servants, guards, lions, a horse and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, and three women lay in her chamber. Woolley suspected the retainers had taken poison. CAT scans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> suggest that some were killed by blunt force, and cinnabar was used to slow decomposition. A death pit above her chamber held 21 attendants, a lyre or harp and a chariot, but it is unclear whether the pits relate directly to her. Her remains, including pieces of her damaged skull, are in the Natural History Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and the finds were divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, the Penn Museum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Among about 1,850 burials Woolley counted sixteen or seventeen royal tombs; the unlooted tomb of Queen <b>Puabi</b>, known from a cylinder seal, yielded the Standard of Ur, and the Lyres of Ur, bull-shaped instruments with eleven strings, came to light in 1928.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
